<p>And eight clients:</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Eight clients</th><th>SoliDB</th><th>Memgraph</th><th>Neo4j</th><th>ArangoDB</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>hop1</code></td><td>323,272</td><td>76,294</td><td>37,438</td><td>30,295</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>hop2</code></td><td>150,343</td><td>52,092</td><td>28,481</td><td>9,648</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>hop3</code></td><td>16,213</td><td>13,200</td><td>8,164</td><td>1,324</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>filtered</code></td><td>74,274</td><td>55,131</td><td>29,014</td><td>6,721</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>fof_bought</code></td><td>35,730</td><td>1,396</td><td>14,469</td><td>2,102</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>shortest</code></td><td>112,449</td><td>2.4</td><td>31,701</td><td>25,957</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>SoliDB is ahead on the twelve series. The margin is narrow in one place: three hops at one client, 9% over Memgraph, and three earlier passes of the same series had SoliDB 3 to 5% behind, before the last change described below. On the others it is 12% (the filtered traversal at one client) to several times.</p>
<p>Two of Memgraph's figures need a word. Its shortest path takes 1.8 seconds with the query we wrote for it (<code>*BFS</code>), which as far as we can tell explores from the start until it meets the target, among people known by very many; Neo4j's <code>shortestPath</code> and ArangoDB's <code>SHORTEST_PATH</code> search from both ends. And <code>fof_bought</code> is a pattern with a variable-length part followed by a purchase, which it appears to enumerate path by path. Someone who knows Memgraph better may write both differently; the harness is there to be corrected.</p>
<p>Loading the graph, with its indexes, from eight connections: SoliDB 24 seconds, ArangoDB 27, Neo4j 50, Memgraph 101. SoliDB's figure is with a write buffer of 2 GB, set in the script. With the server's default of 64 MB the load takes 46 seconds, behind ArangoDB: ten million edges, each with four index entries, fill a 64 MB buffer many times a second, and flushing and compacting them is most of the work. The query figures are the same with either buffer.</p>
</section>

<section id="before"><h2>Where 2.10.3 stood</h2>
<p>The first passes ran on a small graph, 20,000 people and 200,000 edges, against the released 2.10.3. One hop was already ahead. Two hops took 560 µs where Neo4j took 121. Three hops took 7.3 ms where Neo4j and Memgraph took about 0.4. A shortest path took 10.8 ms where Neo4j took 0.08, and that was after a first fix: on 2.10.2, every shortest path on a graph of 100,000 edges ran into the query time limit.</p>
<p>Reading the code said why. A shortest path loaded every edge of the collection into memory, then looked through all of them at each step of the search. A traversal read one vertex, then its edges, then the next vertex; each edge was fetched and decoded whole to learn where it led; and each step of the walk carried a copy of the documents of its whole path, whether or not the query asked for the path.</p>
</section>

<section id="changes"><h2>What changed in SoliDB</h2>
<p>In the order the profiles asked for them:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Edges are found from an index that holds both ends.</strong> An edge collection now has, besides its indexes on <code>_from</code> and <code>_to</code>, one on <code>[_from, _to]</code> and one on <code>[_to, _from]</code>. An entry says where an edge leads, so expanding a vertex reads a range of the index and no edge document, unless the query reads the edge.</li>
<li><strong>A breadth-first traversal goes level by level.</strong> The edges of a whole level are read, then its vertices in one batch, and only the fields the query reads are decoded. The filters that follow the traversal are checked as each vertex is found, and a <code>COLLECT</code> that follows is fed directly, without building the rows first.</li>
<li><strong>A shortest path is searched from both ends</strong>, on where the edges lead; the edges of the path found are read at the end. It returns a shortest path, not necessarily the one a search from the start would have met first.</li>
<li><strong>What a traversal reads is kept until the next write.</strong> The neighbours of each vertex walked, with the documents the query read of them, stay in memory, per collection, for as long as the collection is not written. Any committed write to a collection drops what was kept of it, in the same way and at the same moment as it drops the cached query results. The size is bounded (<code>SOLIDB_GRAPH_CACHE_MB</code>), and <code>0</code> turns it off.</li>
</ul>
<p>The first three are algorithmic and hold whatever the workload. On the small graph, with nothing kept between queries, they brought two hops from 560 to 130 µs and three hops from 7.3 to 1.1 ms. The fourth is what took SoliDB from there to the figures above.</p>
</section>

<section id="limits"><h2>What this does not show</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>A read-only benchmark is the best case for what SoliDB keeps in memory.</strong> The harness loads the graph and then only reads it, cycling through 2,000 sets of parameters per scenario: after the warm-up, most of what a query needs was read by an earlier one. A graph that is written all the time would see SoliDB read its indexes at every query, at the figures of the paragraph above rather than those of the tables. We have not measured a mix of reads and writes. Memgraph is favoured the same way by keeping its whole graph in memory, and Neo4j by a page cache as large as the data; ArangoDB reads through RocksDB.</li>
<li><strong>The container gave SoliDB room for it</strong>: 2 GB per kept map, where the server's default is 256 MB, and the 2 GB write buffer mentioned above. At the defaults, a graph this size is only partly kept and loads in 46 seconds. Both settings are in <code>graph.sh</code>, as Neo4j's heap and page cache are.</li>
<li><strong>The questions are small.</strong> Neighbourhoods, a filter, a shortest path. No analytics over the whole graph, no pattern with several branches, no write. Graph databases are built for more than this, and Cypher expresses patterns that SDBQL does not.</li>
<li><strong>The queries are ours.</strong> Each is the form its own documentation suggests, and the two Memgraph figures above show what a different form may be worth.</li>
<li><strong>Eight clients on four cores</strong> is as far as the harness went.</li>
<li><strong>This build is not released.</strong> The figures are those of a branch; they will be measured again on the release that carries it.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section id="run"><h2>Running it</h2>
<div class="code-card post-code"><div class="cc-bar"><span class="dotk"></span> terminal</div><pre>cd benchmarks/compare
cargo build --release --bin solidb-graph
./graph.sh all                       <span class="tok-com"># the four databases, one after another</span>
./graph.sh solidb neo4j              <span class="tok-com"># only those</span>
PEOPLE=20000 PRODUCTS=500 WARM=1 SECS=3 RUNS=1 ./graph.sh all   <span class="tok-com"># a smoke pass</span></pre></div>
<p>The README of <code>benchmarks/compare</code> describes the data, the six scenarios with each engine's query, the settings and what is checked. If one of the queries is not the one you would have written for your database, that is the place to say so.</p>
</section>
</div>
